// Sized to sit inside a 30px virtualized board row alongside the bye and
// salary columns, and sized to its CONTENT, because the row's every-pixel
// competitor is the player name beside it.
//
// This carried a fixed 46px basis until 2026-09-02, justified by a claim that a
// content-sized chip "would ragged the salary column out of alignment". That
// claim is false and was never measured. The bye (20px) and salary (45px)
// columns both have fixed bases and sit at the row's end, so the NAME absorbs
// every change in the chip's width and the salary column does not move:
// measured on the rendered board, its left edge held at 431px across chip
// widths of 28, 35, 39 and 58px. The fixed basis was buying nothing and
// charging the name 46px on every row, including the `$7` chip that needs 28.
//
// It cost the name visibly. On the 188-row board a fixed-46px chip took the
// rows whose name is clipped from 1 to 23, worst case 23px of a name cut off
// with no ellipsis -- `.player__name` is `overflow: hidden` with no
// `text-overflow`, so a clipped name is indistinguishable from a short one.
.auction-election-chip
  flex 0 0 auto
  padding 0 6px
  display flex
  align-items center
  justify-content center
  height 18px
  align-self center
  border-radius 9px
  font-size 10px
  font-weight 800
  cursor pointer
  background $accentColor
  color white
  overflow hidden

  // A decline is not a $0 maximum, and the two must not read alike at a glance.
  // The amount chip is the loud one because it is a live claim; a decline is an
  // answer already given, so it recedes without disappearing.
  &.declined
    background transparent
    color lighten(black, 50%)
    border 1px solid $borderColor
    font-weight 400

  &.settled
    opacity 0.45
    cursor default
